      'Phone booths are designed to use a compact footprint, but they still need clearance for door operation, airflow, electrical access, and safe office circulation. Larger pods need more planning around walkways, sightlines, ceiling height, delivery access, and assembly space.',
      'Do not choose based only on whether the pod physically fits into an empty corner. Confirm how people will enter it, whether surrounding desks will be disturbed, and whether the delivery route can accommodate the components.',
      'The [office pod size guide](/articles/how-to-choose-office-pod-size) provides a model-by-model planning framework.',
      '## Comfort and session length',
      'A smaller phone booth can be comfortable for calls and focused sessions when ventilation is adequate. For longer individual work, a larger worktop and more internal space may be more useful than the smallest possible footprint.',
      'Ace Plus includes 155 CFM airflow and is designed for extended daily use. Ace Flex provides more room for documents, equipment, and longer solo sessions. The right choice depends on how people actually work, not only how many people fit inside.',
      '## Acoustic expectations',
      'Both phone booths and larger office pods are intended to reduce distraction and improve speech privacy. They are not automatically completely soundproof.',
      'Acoustic results depend on panel construction, door seals, glazing, ventilation design, placement, and the surrounding office noise level. Ace Uno uses acoustic PET panels but its supplied product sheet does not publish a dB rating. Request and review model-specific test documentation before relying on any acoustic figure.',
      '## Price differences',
      'Phone booths are usually the lowest-cost entry point because they serve one person and use fewer materials. Larger office pods cost more but can support interviews, meetings, and collaboration that a solo booth cannot.',
